  "result": "I wrote the 10-minute lesson on O3 at `lessons/0001-context-and-memory-in-an-agent.html`. I haven't opened it in a browser or counted its words, so the length is only estimated from the section plan.\n\nIt covers:\n- **The context window:** the model keeps nothing between calls, and the program resends everything each step, which is why cost grows.\n- **Context rot:** why a fuller window is not a better one, and the two ways to trim it, clearing and compaction.\n- **Memory between sessions:** notes as memory, using the memory tool as the example.\n- **Retrieval:** up-front versus just-in-time, applied to the long machine manuals.\n- **Practice:** a worked example in four steps (a student's second visit to the band saw), a faded practice task on booking a 3D printer, two multiple-choice checks and a warm-up recall on the agent loop.\n\nThe lesson cites three Anthropic pages, which are the Anthropic engineering article on context engineering, the context windows docs and the memory tool docs. I opened all three this session, and I added them to a new `RESOURCES.md`, which has no community listed yet. I also added a line to `NOTES.md`.\n\nYou can ask me anything about the lesson, and ask for a quiz (`/quiz`) when you're ready. I didn't write one, as `/teach` doesn't. Quiz 0001 (the agent loop) is still ungraded, so the lesson's file number is separate from that quiz's number.\n\nQuestion for you: your notes say you've heard of \"token\" but can't say what it limits. The lesson gives a one-line definition. Would you like a short follow-up on tokens and cost per request, since your budget is small?",
